{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/apphost at exact clean pushed head d465b450ea3d81a78fe86a7c89d861ad49563f0e on request/config-cross-process-cas, answering 9da2d677 under my promise 91c51d48. This replaces the artifacts I retracted at 2df37050 and follows the sequence 7e7d0c30 set: amend, gate, push, then publish with the mutation commands and results.\n\nTHE DEFECT. save() wrote the whole in-memory Config, the instance loaded when the Workspace opened, and rememberVerifiedFrontier held a per-instance mutex while doing it. The frontier was guarded against rollback; the actor map was guarded by nothing. A process that opened before another granted custody erased it on its next frontier advance. The workroom lost ox-builder and ox-checker four times today with keys and roster memberships intact.\n\nTHE TRANSACTION. MutateConfig takes the exclusive side of an advisory lock on a sidecar file, reads the stored file, refuses if any of the seven immutable scalars diverges, applies only the callers intended change to that fresh state, validates, and renames. LoadConfig takes the shared side, so a read never overlaps a replacement — exclusion is the guarantee rather than any claim about rename atomicity toward open readers, which is what makes it statable on Windows. A mutation that finds no stored file refuses: bootstrap is CreateConfigs exclusive contract, and both init sites use it, so a deletion can no longer be resurrected from a callers memory. The value returned on ErrConfigUnchanged is a clone taken before the callback ran, so writes into the argument die with it. The memory-first frontier fast path is gone. Unsupported platforms refuse both lock sides.\n\nTHE SEAM, AND WHY THE EARLIER ONE DID NOT PROVE ITS CLAIM. The first cross-process tests printed a ready line and then called into the lock, so the parent released before the child had contended and the scheduler decided whether they ever did. Both tests passed with either lock removed. Now lockConfig invokes a test-only witness between opening the sidecar and blocking on it, on the same handle; the child installs a witness that makes a non-blocking attempt at the same lock side and announces only when the attempt is refused because another process holds it. A free lock at that point is an explicit protocol failure, not a pass. So the announcement means a lock attempt was made and rejected by the operating system while the parent held its transaction open with nothing stored. The witness is nil in production and the probes exist only in _test.go files.\n\nTHE MUTATION RESULTS, WITH THEIR ACTUAL FAILURE MODES. Replacing the writers lockConfig(metaDir, lockFileExclusively) with a no-op release: TestMutateConfigSerializesAgainstAnotherProcess fails six runs out of six, in hundredths of a second rather than by timeout, because the childs probe finds the lock free and refuses, so no announcement is ever printed and the parent reports that no contention was witnessed. Replacing LoadConfigs lock with a bare read: TestLoadConfigCoordinatesWithAWriterInAnotherProcess fails six runs out of six, because the probe still observes the parents exclusive hold and announces, but the read then proceeds without waiting and its fresh-state assertion fails since the parent has not stored yet.\n\nThe previous commit message described a different mechanism — that removing either lock removed the witness with it — and codex was right that this is false: lockConfig is still called, so the witness always runs. The message now names the two modes above. That correction is why this head exists.\n\nWHAT I CANNOT PROVE, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED. I do not prove the childs blocking acquisition was already asleep at the moment of release; there is an instruction window between the refused probe and the blocking call, and no portable API observes flock waiters. Nothing in the code or the message claims otherwise. The mutation demonstration covers removing each lock acquisition, which is what the verdict asked for; a different weakening — keeping lockConfig but no-opping lockFileShared itself — would let the reader child announce and then race, and that variant is not deterministic. The writer-side equivalent is caught. The Windows lock is compile-verified only. The unsupported-platform path is compile-verified through js/wasm and fail-closed by construction, not executed. And one mutation runs green: reverting the init sites from CreateConfig to SaveConfig is pinned by nothing, because the window has no injection seam.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD after the amendment rather than transferred from the head before it. gofmt -l . empty, git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, GOOS=windows go build ./... clean, GOOS=js GOARCH=wasm go build ./... clean, go test -race over internal/app and internal/apphost clean, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log.","body":{"commit":"d465b450ea3d81a78fe86a7c89d861ad49563f0e","path":"internal/apphost"}}